<script lang="ts">
  import DocLayout from "#lib/ui/doc/DocLayout.svelte";
  import DocHead from "#lib/ui/doc/Head.svelte";
  import DocBlock from "#lib/ui/doc/Block.svelte";
  import Code from "#lib/ui/doc/CodeBlock.svelte";
  import Example from "#lib/ui/doc/Example.svelte";
  import ConfigComponent from "#lib/ui/doc/ConfigComponent.svelte";
  import { processID, editURL } from "#lib";
  let { component }: {component: any } = $props();

  import { Form, Input, Button } from "theui-svelte";
</script>

<DocLayout>
  <DocHead title="Form" text="A reusable wrapper for the HTML form element, simplifying form creation, submission handling, event management and controlling all the inputs from a single place." edit_url={editURL("form")} />

  <DocBlock title="About">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>Form</code> component is a wrapper around the native <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> element. You do not have to use it, but it is where the shared input settings live: set <code>variant</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>rounded</code> or <code>floatingLabel</code> once on the form and every field inside picks them up.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Example">
    <Example>
      <p class="not-prose">To use the <b>Form</b> component, simply import it from the library and wrap your form inputs inside it. Here's a basic example:</p>
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<script>
  import { Form } from "theui-svelte";
</script>

<Form>
  <!-- Add your form inputs here -->
</Form>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
    <p class="not-prose">Every input inside it picks up those settings, so a change in one place reaches the whole form.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Form Method">
    <Example>
      <p class="not-prose">The <code>method</code> prop specifies the HTTP method to be used when the form is submitted. It corresponds to the native HTML <code>method</code> attribute of the <b>&lt;form&gt;</b> element. Available values are <code>GET</code> and <code>POST</code>. Default <code>POST</code>.</p>
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<Form method="GET">
  <!-- Form inputs go here -->
</Form>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
      <p class="not-prose">In this example, the form will use the <code>GET</code> method when submitted, appending the form data to the URL. If no <code>method</code> is specified, it defaults to <code>POST</code>.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Shared Input Settings">
    <Example>
      <p class="not-prose">The <code>Form</code> component passes these props down to all the inputs inside it, so you can style every input from one place:</p>
      <ul class="my-0">
        <li><span class="not-prose"><code>variant</code></span>: <code>"bordered"</code> (default) or <code>"flat"</code>.</li>
        <li><span class="not-prose"><code>size</code></span>: <code>"sm"</code>, <code>"md"</code> (default), <code>"lg"</code> or <code>"xl"</code>.</li>
        <li><span class="not-prose"><code>floatingLabel</code></span>: shows the label inside the input and moves it up on focus. It is <code>true</code> by default for the <code>"flat"</code> variant and <code>false</code> otherwise.</li>
        <li><span class="not-prose"><code>rounded</code></span>: the border radius of the inputs. The default is <code>"md"</code>.</li>
        <li><span class="not-prose"><code>animationSpeed</code></span>: the transition speed of the inputs and floating labels. The default is <code>"normal"</code>.</li>
        <li><span class="not-prose"><code>labelClasses</code></span>: custom classes for all the input labels.</li>
        <li><span class="not-prose"><code>reset</code></span>: removes the default input styles so only your own classes apply. The default is <code>false</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="not-prose">A <code>Fieldset</code> inside the form can override these settings for its own inputs, and each input can override them with its own props.</p>
      {#snippet example()}
        <Form class="w-full max-w-sm" variant="flat" size="lg">
          <Input name="name">Your name</Input>
          <Input name="email" type="email">Email address</Input>
          <Button type="submit">Submit</Button>
        </Form>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<Form variant="flat" size="lg">
  <Input name="name">Your name</Input>
  <Input name="email" type="email">Email address</Input>
  <Button type="submit">Submit</Button>
</Form>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Progressive Enhancement">
    <Example>
      <p class="not-prose">The <code>enhance</code> prop accepts a Svelte action that is applied to the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> element. Pass SvelteKit's <code>enhance</code> action to submit the form without a full page reload, while keeping it working when JavaScript is unavailable.</p>
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<script>
  import { enhance } from "$app/forms";
  import { Form } from "theui-svelte";
</script>

<Form action="?/login" {enhance}>
  <!-- Form inputs go here -->
</Form>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Accessibility">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>Form</code> component renders a native <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> element, which browsers and screen readers already understand. Any <b>ARIA</b> role, label or attribute you pass is spread onto it, so you can add whatever a particular page needs.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Configuration">
    <p class="not-prose">Some of the <code>Form</code> props are not used by the form element itself. They are handed down to the inputs inside it, so you set them once instead of on every field.</p>
    <ConfigComponent {component} />
  </DocBlock>

